.TH CLADENC-REPORT 1 "" cladenc "cladenc"
.SH NAME
cladenc\-report \- print the report of a directed evolution that has ended, without changing it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBcladenc report\fP
    [\fB\-\-help\fP]
    [\fB\-\-json\fP]
    \fB\-\-outputs\-dir\fP <DIR>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBcladenc report\fP is for a directed evolution that has ended.
It reads the journal in the outputs directory and prints the report of the
directed evolution on standard output, without changing it: the model, how
many procreations were started, the best harness, how many of the selection
tasks, of the corroboration tasks and of the non-holdout tasks at least one
harness completed, and for each harness its progenitor, whether it was
created, which reads unchanged where its procreation left the copy of the
source code as it was, how many of the selection tasks it completed and did
not complete, how many of the corroboration tasks it completed and did not
complete, how many of the non-holdout tasks it completed and did not complete,
the counts of the selection tasks for its clade, which is the harness and all
its descendants together, and how many seconds the harness ran in all its
trials together and how many its progenitor ran in the procreation that made
it.
.PP
Nothing is chosen by the corroboration tasks, so the counts of the
corroboration tasks are where a person reads how far the best harness is ahead
of the primogenitor.
\fBcladenc\-start\fP(1) says what the selection tasks, the corroboration tasks
and the non-holdout tasks are.
.PP
Given a directed evolution that has not ended, \fBcladenc report\fP prints
nothing on standard output: it says on standard error that the directed
evolution has not ended and that \fBcladenc status\fP is the sub-command to
call, and exits with status 2.
.SH FLAGS
.TP
\fB\-h\fP, \fB\-\-help\fP
Print help.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fP
Print one JSON object in place of the table.
.TP
\fB\-\-outputs\-dir\fP <DIR>
The outputs directory of the directed evolution to report on.
.SH THE BEST HARNESS AND THE PRIMOGENITOR
\fBcladenc report\fP says three things of the best harness and the
primogenitor, counted on the corroboration tasks: how many of them only the
best harness completed, how many only the primogenitor completed, and how
likely luck alone gives a lead at least as large.
A corroboration task that both harnesses completed, or that neither completed,
says nothing about which of the two is ahead, and is left out.
Where two harnesses are equally good, a task that only one of them completed
is as likely the one's as the other's, like the toss of a fair coin.
So the likelihood is that of as many heads or more in as many tosses as there
are tasks that tell the two apart: with 14 tasks that only the best harness
completed and 6 that only the primogenitor completed, 14 heads or more in 20
tosses, which is 0.058, about 6 times in a hundred.
Where no task tells the two apart the likelihood is 1, and where the best
harness is the primogenitor there are no two harnesses to compare.
cladenc draws no line through the likelihood: what counts as more than luck is
for the person who reads it to say.
The likelihood is shown for the best harness alone, since among many harnesses
one or two show a low likelihood by luck.
.SH EXPERIMENTS
\fBcladenc report\fP and \fBcladenc status\fP say of the experiments how many
were conducted, in how many the progenitor's child was created, in how many
the singleton control group harness was created, and in how many both were.
Over the experiments where both were created, each counts the corroboration
tasks that only the progenitor's child completed and those that only the
singleton control group harness completed, and says how likely luck alone
gives a lead at least as large where the two are created equally well, counted
as it is for the best harness and the primogenitor.
A corroboration task that one of the two has had no trial on yet is left out.
In the table, the row of a singleton control group harness names no
progenitor, and names under experiment the progenitor's child of its
experiment.
The singleton control group harnesses are left out of the count of the tasks
that at least one harness completed.
Where no experiment created both the progenitor's child and the singleton
control group harness, each says so in the place of those counts and of the
likelihood, and \fB\-\-json\fP gives no likelihood for the experiments.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBcladenc\fP(1),
\fBcladenc\-resume\fP(1),
\fBcladenc\-start\fP(1),
\fBcladenc\-status\fP(1)
